// The seven operators whose ANSWER depends on the operands' sign: / % >>
// and the four order comparisons. Everything else (+ - * & | ^ == !=) gives
// the same bits either way and is covered by arith.csp.
//
// U holds 0xFFFFFFF7 -- 4294967287 unsigned, -9 as a signed int. That is the
// number the runtime used to get wrong: `U % 10` returned -9 (printed back
// through the unsigned type as 4294967287) instead of 7, because eval_op only
// had signed arms and the declared type never reached the instruction.
//
// U and V are VARIABLES, so these run through the runtime evaluator. The
// constant folder takes the same arm (eval2 builds the instruction it would
// have emitted) and Rfold below checks it.
#variable U:32 unsigned = 0xFFFFFFF7
#variable V:32 unsigned = 10
#variable S = 0

#variable Rrem:32  unsigned = 0
#variable Rdiv:32  unsigned = 0
#variable Rsra:32  unsigned = 0
#variable Rmix:32  unsigned = 0
#variable Rlt   = 0
#variable Rgt   = 0
#variable Rlte  = 0
#variable Rgte  = 0

// One unsigned operand makes the whole operation unsigned, so the plain `10`
// on the right of Rmix and of Kfold does not drag either back to signed.
//
// Kbig carries the type: a bare `0xFFFFFFF7` is an INT literal and folds
// SIGNED, the same way a C `int` literal would.
#constant Kbig:32 unsigned = 0xFFFFFFF7
#constant Kfold = Kbig % 10
#variable Rfold:32 unsigned = Kfold

Rrem = U % V,   S=1 ? S==0
Rdiv = U / V,   S=2 ? S==1
Rsra = U >> 1,  S=3 ? S==2
Rlt  = U < V,   S=4 ? S==3
Rgt  = U > V,   S=5 ? S==4
Rlte = U <= V,  S=6 ? S==5
Rgte = U >= V,  S=7 ? S==6
Rmix = (U + 2) % 10, S=8 ? S==7
